#539 is not closed. The fix is queued to land on main but had no verdict when my time ran out (queued at 11:46Z, still waiting at 14:52Z). The manager will land it after this run, and the next run will post the outcome comment and close the issue.

**The fix (`fa71c8aa`, test only):** it changes `pkg/templates/java/chassis/diskstore/DiskStoreTest.java.tmpl`.
- `testAppendsDoNotWaitForUploads` now does 5 appends with a 60s deadline instead of 100 within 20s. An append that waits on the shipper waits until the sink recovers, which never happens while every upload fails, so the test still catches blocking but no longer measures how fast the disk can fsync.
- Two new checks: the upload is still failing when the appends finish (so the check isn't empty), and no append threw.
- The cleanup at the end stops the writer and lets the sink succeed before closing the log, so a failure prints one FAIL line and no `NullPointerException`.

**Verification**
- **Clean run:** `just current-generation-java` ended `==> Current cross-test generation and compile passed`.
- **By-hand check:** I made `RedoLog.append` wait for `shipper.pending() == 0`. The test then printed only `FAIL: appends must not wait on a failing upload`, with no NPE.
- **Under disk load:** neither `fio` nor `stress-ng` is installed, so I used parallel writers that sync every write to the same disk. DiskStoreTest passed every time: 5 runs on its own, once inside the full Java leg, and on each release line. This disk is fast, though: 100 fsyncs took 0.9s under load, so the old test also passed. The nightly failure was not reproduced here.
- **Full Java leg under load:** it failed both times, but in a different self-test, `AuthorizerBehaviorTest`: `JWK watch made 1 attempts in 2.5s ... want 2..4`, then `0 attempts` on the second run. That check counts attempts in a fixed 2.5s from startup, and a slow start eats that time. I filed it as #540; it isn't part of this fix.
- **`just gates`:** not finished. I stopped it at 14:50Z for time, so there is no `OVERALL` line. Before that, `protoc-check`, `soundness` and `test` ended `rc=0`, and `DiskStoreTest: all checks passed` in its `current-generation` leg. The land's own gate run will give the verdict.

**Release:** main has taken features since v2.6.1 (#321), so main gets no `v2.6.2` tag. The fix goes to the release branches instead. Each PR is a cherry-pick of `fa71c8aa` and names the tag to cut once it merges:

| Line | PR | Tag to cut |
|---|---|---|
| release/2.1 | #541 | v2.1.21 |
| release/2.2 | #542 | v2.2.18 |
| release/2.3 | #543 | v2.3.20 |
| release/2.4 | #544 | v2.4.20 |
| release/2.5 | #545 | v2.5.21 |
| release/2.6 | #546 | v2.6.10 |

release/2.0 doesn't have this test. No service handlers were touched, so the trace rule doesn't apply. My last checkpoint on `looper/tacplus-539` records all of this for the next run.
